==English==

===Etymology===
In [[w:Mary Shelly|Mary Shelley]]'s novel ''Frankenstein'', Dr. Frankenstein assembled a [[monster]] from human corpses; it eventually escaped his control.

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper-noun|head=[[Frankenstein]][[-'s|'s]] [[monster]]}}

# {{w|lang=en|Frankenstein's monster}}, the creature from Mary Shelley's 1818 novel

===Noun===
{{en-noun|head=[[Frankenstein]][[-'s|'s]] [[monster]]}}

# A thing that is [[cobble]]d together from parts of other things.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1991|author=Euan George Nisbet|title=Living Earth: A Short History of Life and Its Home|page=84
|passage=Like the English language, the eukaryote cell is a chimera, a '''Frankenstein's monster''', assembled from bits and pieces of genetic information...}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author=Roger Horrocks; Jo Campling|title=Male Myths and Icons: Masculinity in Popular Culture|page=141
|passage=He is like a '''Frankenstein's monster''' in reverse: everything that is pretty is combined together to produce a perfect androgyne.}}
# A creation that overpowers or slips out of the control of its creator, often proceeding to turn on its creator or harm others.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1968|author=w:Harold Joseph Laski|title=Reflections on the Revolution of Our Time|page=109
|passage=They created a '''Frankenstein's monster''' which they did not imagine could grow out of their control.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1977|author=Norman R. Augustine|title=Augustine's Laws|page=68
|passage=Somehow, the law does not always seem to serve those who created it, becoming at times a '''Frankenstein's monster''' of sorts.}}

